Author: jingjiangze <1689170897@qq.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 10 21:43:02 2026 +0800

    test: compact public-frame acceptance on unite and boss fields
    
    The protocol acceptance test for the compact m.public layer (PR #157): a real
    co-op match, production client-side combat, driven through a unite round
    (3 humans, the unite spec on the real sim) and the boss pair plus the hidden
    round (2 humans), asserting:
      * the baseline carries the ten per-match constants and the hot frame drops
        exactly them while keeping the hot state;
      * the client's exact merge (public/js/main.js wireNet) over
        [baseline, ...hot frames] agrees with publicView() (full) at the same
        match state on every key the full view publishes - nothing a compact
        frame carried is erased, the constants included;
      * a unite-phase frame carries v.unite { helpers, leakers } and the merge
        keeps it;
      * the boss round's frame names the boss field (kind boss, then hidden) with
        its shared pool, the b.start spec carries its units, and the merge keeps
        bossId / hiddenBossId (the 'the boss does not show' guard);
      * the Lobby's routing: a session without hello.pub gets the full encoding
        of every m.public broadcast, pubCap > 0 the compact one (and an
        all-capable room never builds the full frame).
    
    Finding, reported with the PR: the strict deep-equal the brief asked for
    cannot hold with the client's merge, because {...prev, ...next} never removes
    a key - after a phase that published a phase-scoped pocket ends (draft, sp,
    unite, uniteResult, ...) the mirror keeps it while the fresh full view no
    longer carries it. The test asserts the strongest property that does hold
    (every published key deep-equal, extra keys only pockets the wire carried)
    and records the consequence in the header: a stale uniteResult stays readable
    by the client's SETTLE effect.

